1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,870
This is Dot Goose, part one. We will follow eight
small C sharp programs through the Goose compiler.

2
00:00:07,090 --> 00:00:14,490
For each numbered example, compare the code and the
output. At the end, we will compare executable sizes.

3
00:00:15,175 --> 00:00:16,475
First example.

4
00:00:17,125 --> 00:00:18,705
A first translation.

5
00:00:19,185 --> 00:00:24,645
Explanation: This program prints Dot Goose, starts
with forty, and adds two.

6
00:00:24,865 --> 00:00:30,325
The C sharp integer becomes I thirty two in Goose, and
Write Line becomes print.

7
00:00:31,008 --> 00:00:34,908
The condition still checks for forty-two before
printing the value.

8
00:00:35,128 --> 00:00:41,738
Both runs also print the wrapped result from the final
addition. We will unpack that in chapter four.

9
00:00:42,425 --> 00:00:43,725
Second example.

10
00:00:44,375 --> 00:00:45,825
Console escapes.

11
00:00:46,305 --> 00:00:50,335
Explanation: Look at the strings between the quotation
marks.

12
00:00:50,555 --> 00:00:57,615
Only the call changes. The escape sequences for the
tab, quotes, backslash, and line breaks carry through.

13
00:00:58,300 --> 00:01:04,290
The first print writes an empty line. Later, carriage
return and newline remain distinct.

14
00:01:04,510 --> 00:01:10,250
The test compares raw output bytes, so even a change a
terminal might hide would fail.

15
00:01:10,925 --> 00:01:12,205
Third example.

16
00:01:12,855 --> 00:01:14,365
Loops and branches.

17
00:01:14,845 --> 00:01:22,245
Explanation: The index starts at zero and stops before
five. The running total starts at negative two.

18
00:01:22,465 --> 00:01:28,485
At index two, the else branch changes the Boolean flag
instead of adding to the total.

19
00:01:29,175 --> 00:01:35,395
The remaining additions take the total to six. The
final condition checks the total and the flag.

20
00:01:35,615 --> 00:01:40,185
Goose keeps the same loop and branch structure. Both
runs print six.

21
00:01:40,842 --> 00:01:42,162
Fourth example.

22
00:01:42,812 --> 00:01:44,512
Integer overflow.

23
00:01:44,992 --> 00:01:50,672
Explanation: This example adds one to the largest
signed thirty-two-bit integer.

24
00:01:50,892 --> 00:01:56,092
Unchecked C sharp arithmetic wraps around, giving the
smallest signed integer.

25
00:01:56,758 --> 00:02:03,388
The Dot Goose helper adds the same bits as unsigned
values, then casts back to a signed integer.

26
00:02:03,608 --> 00:02:09,008
That preserves all three results even when Goose runs
with debug checks enabled.

27
00:02:09,675 --> 00:02:10,891
Fifth example.

28
00:02:11,541 --> 00:02:13,354
From classes to structs.

29
00:02:13,834 --> 00:02:20,687
Explanation: The scoped Counter class becomes a Goose
struct. The factory named new Counter takes the

30
00:02:20,687 --> 00:02:22,112
constructor argument.

31
00:02:22,332 --> 00:02:28,390
Add still looks like a method call. Its writable
receiver lets it update the counter in place.

32
00:02:29,050 --> 00:02:34,106
Point is a value type. Copying it gives the program an
independent value.

33
00:02:34,326 --> 00:02:41,629
Changing the copy leaves the original sum at thirteen.
The copy prints five, after the counter prints forty-

34
00:02:41,629 --> 00:02:41,899
two.

35
00:02:42,550 --> 00:02:43,894
Sixth example.

36
00:02:44,544 --> 00:02:46,357
Defaults and struct copies.

37
00:02:46,837 --> 00:02:54,335
Explanation: The explicit Point constructor sets X and
marks the point ready. The parameterless construction

38
00:02:54,335 --> 00:02:55,307
uses defaults.

39
00:02:55,527 --> 00:02:59,495
That means zero for both coordinates and false for the
ready flag.

40
00:03:00,175 --> 00:03:07,642
Changing the blank point's Y coordinate does not set
its flag. Its Value method still returns negative one.

41
00:03:07,862 --> 00:03:15,115
The assigned copy can change independently. The final
values are seven, negative one, and nine.

42
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:17,101
Seventh example.

43
00:03:17,751 --> 00:03:19,714
Same name, different receiver.

44
00:03:20,194 --> 00:03:27,204
Explanation: Point and Counter each declare a method
called Read. The receiver type tells the compiler

45
00:03:27,204 --> 00:03:28,578
which method to use.

46
00:03:28,798 --> 00:03:33,534
Goose represents those methods as functions with
distinct receiver types.

47
00:03:34,217 --> 00:03:40,681
Point reads seven. Counter reads twenty-one, and Read
Twice calls the counter version two times.

48
00:03:40,901 --> 00:03:48,581
Setting the enabled flag to false changes the counter
to negative one. Both runs produce the same sequence.

49
00:03:49,258 --> 00:03:50,624
Eighth example.

50
00:03:51,274 --> 00:03:53,834
Evaluation order with side effects.

51
00:03:54,314 --> 00:04:01,226
Explanation: The counter starts at ten. The left side
of the sum calls Next first, printing eleven.

52
00:04:01,446 --> 00:04:06,886
Then the two arguments to Take call Next again,
printing twelve and thirteen in that order.

53
00:04:07,550 --> 00:04:13,118
Take prints its first argument, twelve, and returns
its second argument, thirteen.

54
00:04:13,338 --> 00:04:17,349
The sum prints twenty-four, and the counter still
holds thirteen.

55
00:04:17,569 --> 00:04:24,203
Matching only the final value would miss a reordered
print. The test compares the entire sequence.

56
00:04:24,883 --> 00:04:28,297
Here are the file sizes for the eight Linux examples.

57
00:04:28,517 --> 00:04:33,423
The Goose build with system C libraries is fourteen
point six kilobytes.

58
00:04:33,643 --> 00:04:37,441
Dot net Native A O T is one point one megabytes.

59
00:04:37,661 --> 00:04:43,997
The compressed, self-contained dot net single-file
build is thirty-nine point two megabytes.

60
00:04:44,217 --> 00:04:48,889
The smaller managed D L L uses an installed dot net
runtime.

61
00:04:49,550 --> 00:04:56,788
The fourteen point six kilobyte Goose build bundles
the Goose runtime and still loads the system C

62
00:04:56,788 --> 00:04:57,379
library.

63
00:04:57,599 --> 00:05:04,714
We also built a fully static version, around nine
hundred fifty-five kilobytes. It includes the C

64
00:05:04,714 --> 00:05:05,301
library.

65
00:05:05,521 --> 00:05:11,793
All eight static examples ran in an empty Linux root
with only the executable present.

66
00:05:12,013 --> 00:05:17,495
These examples can use a Docker scratch image on
compatible Kubernetes nodes.

67
00:05:17,715 --> 00:05:22,302
A real backend may still need files such as T L S
certificates.

68
00:05:22,967 --> 00:05:29,623
All eight programs matched in the test suite, both as
Goose executables and in Goose debug mode.

69
00:05:29,843 --> 00:05:35,752
The source, compiler, test suite, and build commands
are in the Dot Goose repository.
